<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarala Das</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarala Das was a 15th-century Odia poet, often called the father of Odia literature. Writing in the reign of the Gajapati king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilendra-deva">Kapilendra Deva</a>, he composed an Odia version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> that freely retells the epic with local legends and settings, as well as the Vilanka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the Chandi Purana. His work established Odia as a literary language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarala-das.jpg" alt="Sarala Das" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prateek Pattanaik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Sarala_Dasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sarala <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dasa">Dasa</a> was born Siddheswara Parida, of the Chasa community, at a village called Kanakavati Patana, known as Kanakapura, at Tentuliapada in what is now Jagatsinghpur district. The date of his birth cannot be fixed, and little is known of his early life. He had no organised schooling, and what he learnt by himself was attributed to the grace of Sarala, goddess of devotion and inspiration; the name Sarala Dasa means servant of Sarala. A story like those told of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> relates that as a boy ploughing his father's field he sang so sweetly that the goddess stopped to listen and gave him the gift of poetry.</p><p>Several passages of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> suggest that he served as a soldier in the army of the Gajapati king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and the battles, places and events he knew from that service went into his writing. He spent his last years at Bila Sarala; a religious house called Munigoswain at his birthplace marks the traditional spot where he composed his works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Besides the Sarala Mahabharata, the Vilanka Ramayana and the Chandi Purana, he wrote the Laxmi Narayana Vachanika. His Mahabharata opens with an invocation to Lord Jagannatha of Puri which shows that he began it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilendra-deva">Kapilendra Deva</a> (1435–67). It keeps the outline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epic but adds many stories of his own, so that it reads as a new creation. The Chandi Purana retells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>'s killing of the buffalo demon Mahishasura with its own departures, and his earliest work, the Vilanka Ramayana, tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>'s fight with the thousand-headed Ravana.</p><p>He wrote in dandi chand, a metre in which the number of letters in a verse is not fixed, and the two lines of a couplet rhyme without being of equal length, as in the Odia folk songs of dances such as the Ghoda-nacha and Sakhinacha. His verse is plain and musical, uses colloquial words and avoids <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskritisation">Sanskritisation</a>. War rather than love is its main mood, and he said he wrote for the benefit of ordinary people, so that religious books could be read in a language all understood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarala Dasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prataparudra Deva hosted the saint Chaitanya, and Odia literature flourished with <b>Sarala Das's</b> Mahabharata.</blockquote><a href="/state/gajapati-kingdom">Gajapati Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jagatsinghpur district, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.27,86.17&amp;year=1464" aria-label="Jagatsinghpur district on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6872 1414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sarala Das"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7322 1684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7322 1684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.27,86.17&amp;year=1464">What was here in 1464? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
